ear 90-95%", "Thin polling shows only a narrow 3-point lead, keeping some uncertainty"], "rationale": "The fundamentals, expert consensus, and markets all point to a Vasquez win, and his money edge is substantial. The lone recent poll shows a tighter race than the ratings imply, so the margin is trimmed slightly but stays firmly in Likely D territory.", "watch": "A late wave of outside spending against Vasquez or a fresh poll showing the race tied would move this toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Southern New Mexico's swing district has flipped from safely red to fiercely competitive, but first-term incumbent Gabe Vasquez enters the final stretch with clear advantages. He has raised $3.8 million to challenger Greg Cunningham's $1.5 million and holds a cash-on-hand edge of more than $1.5 million, while Democratic outside groups are active on his behalf. National attention and attack ads have flooded the district, and Buttigieg campaigned with him in Las Cruces.\n\nRaters overwhelmingly call the race Likely or Lean Democratic, and prediction markets price Vasquez's chances near 90 percent or higher. The only recent independent poll, from early September, showed a narrower 3-point lead, which tempers the expected margin. The race remains competitive enough that a strong closing argument or late spending surge could tighten it, but the weight of evidence favors the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8688, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8708, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.87, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8306, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8384, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8302, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8733, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8614, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026